const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Scientific Poster Preparation Service","serviceType":"Conference poster design, content synthesis and data visualisation","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/publication-support/poster-preparation/","description":"Conference posters built from your manuscript by a domain editor: figures rebuilt at A0 board size and 300 dpi, readable from two metres away.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What poster size should I use, and what are the standard dimensions?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A0 is the usual size outside North America — 841 × 1189 mm, in either orientation — while 36 × 48 inches is the common US equivalent. A1 appears at smaller meetings and many congresses now run e-posters on 16:9 screens instead. None of that matters as much as what your own event says: dimensions and orientation are always stated in the poster instructions, they vary more than people expect, and a board printed to the wrong size cannot be hung."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What font sizes should a research poster use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As a working guide: title 60 to 90 pt, section headings 36 to 44 pt, body text 24 to 32 pt, and captions no smaller than 18 pt. The principle behind the numbers is reading distance — a title has to be findable from five metres in a row of boards, and body text has to be readable from two. Anything below about 20 pt on A0 is decoration rather than communication, and the commonest failure is body text left at 14 pt because it looked fine on a laptop."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I turn my research paper into a conference poster?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By deciding what the poster is about, which is usually one finding rather than the whole study, and then removing everything that does not serve it. A five-thousand-word manuscript becomes four to six hundred words: the question, the design in a line, the primary result with its numbers, and the conclusion. The literature review goes, the secondary analyses go, the reference list shrinks to three or four. Put the detail behind a QR code so nothing is actually lost — it is simply not on the board."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much text should a poster carry?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Four hundred to six hundred words on A0 is comfortable; eight hundred is the point at which people stop reading and start skimming for the conclusion. A billboard-style board carrying one large finding and a single figure can work on two hundred. The test is not whether the text fits but whether someone can take the point in thirty seconds standing up, because that is how long you have before they move to the next board."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I design a scientific poster that works?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A reading order the eye follows without being told — columns read down, or a clear left-to-right flow — a strong type hierarchy so title, headings and body are obviously different, generous white space, and visuals given more room than text. Put the finding high and large rather than at the end where a paper would put it. And leave the board emptier than feels comfortable: white space is what makes the parts you kept legible."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What should a poster actually include?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Title, authors and affiliations, the objective, a short methods statement, the key results with figures, and a conclusion. Then the parts people forget: funding and conflict of interest declarations, ethics approval or trial registration where applicable, a contact address or QR code, and institutional logos if your employer requires them. A full reference list is rarely worth the space — three or four citations and a QR code to the rest does the same job."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which tools are posters designed in?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"PowerPoint is the most common and perfectly capable if the page is set to the real board size before anything is placed on it, which is the step most people skip. Illustrator and InDesign give better typographic control and cleaner print output; LaTeX with a poster class suits mathematical and physics work. The tool matters far less than the discipline: whatever it is drawn in, a poster is designed at full size and then judged at a tenth of it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I reuse the figures from my manuscript?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Rarely without work. A figure exported for an 85 mm journal column has type and line weights sized for that width; enlarged to fill a third of an A0 board it becomes soft, and its labels end up proportionally tiny. Rebuilding from the source data at board size fixes both, and usually the figure should be simplified as well — an eleven-row forest plot that works on the page is too much for someone reading standing up."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does presenting a poster count as prior publication?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Presenting at a scientific meeting, and publishing an abstract in its proceedings, is generally not treated as prior publication and most journals say so explicitly. A full paper in a conference proceedings volume is a different matter and can be. Check the target journal’s policy before the congress rather than after it, and mention the presentation in your cover letter when you submit — editors treat disclosure as normal and non-disclosure as a problem."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the common mistakes in poster design?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Too much text, in that order of importance. After that: type below the legibility floor, no visual hierarchy so everything competes equally, figures pulled straight from the manuscript at the wrong resolution, a title that describes the topic rather than the finding, the conclusion buried bottom-right where a paper would put it, dark backgrounds that print badly and read worse under conference lighting, and colour doing work that a colour-blind reader cannot see."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Publication support","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/publication-support/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Poster preparation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/publication-support/poster-preparation/"}]}];

